# Use the bfq IO scheduler on rotational disks.
#
# Why: this box has 2x 7200 RPM SATA disks (~150-250 combined IOPS) shared
# between ~49 containers and CI. Measured 2026-09-23 with two concurrent CI
# jobs: sdb at 79% utilisation, io pressure full avg60 ~38. Under that load
# mq-deadline lets a throughput-heavy writer starve interactive IO -- during
# the 2026-08-31 incident SSH became unreachable while ping still answered.
# bfq gives interactive processes a fair share, and it is what makes `ionice`
# actually do anything (the forgejo-runner-prune unit already sets
# IOSchedulingClass=idle, which mq-deadline ignores).
#
# KERNEL=="sd[a-z]" deliberately, NOT a bare ATTR{queue/rotational}=="1":
# on this host dm-0..dm-3 (LVM on top of md1) and loop0..loop17+ (snap mounts)
# ALSO report rotational=1, and setting a scheduler on those is at best
# pointless. Physical disks only.
ACTION=="add|change", SUBSYSTEM=="block", KERNEL=="sd[a-z]", ATTR{queue/rotational}=="1", ATTR{queue/scheduler}="bfq"
